Date: Tue, 11 May 93 18:00:51 CDT
From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Subject: AI: Synaptic Storage Requirements

Paul Cisek complains:

> Frankly, I find such numerology of little use. [...]
> Sorry to be so critical, but I find such speculative estimates both
> unrealistic and completely uninformative.

1) Unrealistic.  Well, that's a damning criticism if true -- but any
such criticism is quite welcome because presumably it is backed by
a reason, which can then hopefully be used to make the estimates more
realistic.  Surely you understand the point:  We wish to accurately
model the brain with a nonbiological computational device.  Doing so
requires abstracting away nonessential elements of the physics of
brains because we don't wish to do molecular level simulation and
even that involves simplifying assumptions to make it tractable.

Given this desire, we are forced to wonder what abstractions are
possible that would retain the important aspects of brain function.
A first approximation ignores for the most part diffuse chemical
cues because electrical activity through dendrites and synapses and
so on appears (from experiment) to be more important.  I believe
that other aspects will have to be taken into account, but there's
no reason I can think of to suspect that it will add an order
of magnitude to the difficulty of the task.

So if we're interested in the properties of neurons, we are naturally
led to the question of how many important bits are necessary
to describe a neuron's instantaneous state?  That is, how many bits
are required to specify its functional properties from among the
various possibilities in a sufficient class of neuron models.

Now, we try to do our best; if the results are unrealistic, I'd
really appreciate you pointing out why.

2) Uninformative.  By this, are you just repeating the "inaccurate"
implied by the first objection, or do you think that the answers
(if accurate) don't mean anything?  Why would you say that?

> A related question:  "How big of a balloon is needed to go to the Moon?"

Ah -- now I see, maybe.  That question doesn't have any answer.*
Are you implying that there IS no answer to the question of how
much computational resources would be required for an adequate
simulation of a brain?  Why?

derek
* well, it _may_ be theoretically possible to build a compressed-air
rocket that could achieve orbital velocity [I wouldn't bet on it ever
getting done, though] -- but that's stretching the definition of "balloon"...


