You didn't mean a "winch"-like cable at all.  You meant a "whip-like"
antenna structure.

I would further clarify the contention that a tower is in compression
below GEO and in tension above GEO as follows:

To return to Physics 101, the cable "tether" can not be always totally
in tension since it must impart some angular momentum into the
"elevator" as it rises above GEO.  As with any beam "under-load", a
portion of the "tether" will be in tension and another in compression
until the "correct" angular (orbital) velocity is achieved.  The reason
for the "thicker" cable at GEO is for a "greater moment" not greater
tension.  At extreme orbits more and more of the tower below GEO would
come into tension as the weight of the tower above was "balanced" by
the angular momentum of the elevator and tower above.

It appears that the failing is in our written language once again. 8^)

