Subject: Answering Anton

D Anton Sherwood <dasher@well.sf.ca.us> replies to my post:

>> However, the empiricist concept of tabula rasa that Locke advanced in his
>> refutation of the med[ie]val concept of innate ideas is about the mind,
>> not the emotions or the reflexes.  . . .
>
>Please define and defend this dichotomy.  To me it looks more like a
>grayscale.  Paraphrasing Greg Chalfin, everything is just nerves doin'
>stuff.
>
>What do you call it if the reasoning parts of your mind choose to rely, for
>a particular input, on instinctive parts?  Or does the question itself imply
>a bad paradigm? 

The position is that there is a distinction between concepts and percepts that
is one in kind, not one of degree.  Concepts aren't highly complex percepts,
and percepts aren't simple concepts.

Sensations we have in common with plants and beasts.  Percepts we have in
common with beasts, and concepts we have in common with each other only that
I'm aware of.

Sensations are what are transmitted be nerves, then automatically integrated
into percepts in beasts and ourselves.  These are the material which we
can organize into concepts if we so choose.

Good question!  I hope this helps answer it.

