Date: Wed, 12 May 1993 11:15:02 -0400
From: "Perry E. Metzger" <pmetzger@lehman.com>
Subject: The Game of Go and the Wasting of Time? 
X-Reposting-Policy: redistribute only with permission

Timothy C. May says:
> 
> >   I used to play go a little; chess, more; checkers, still more.
> > My current opinion is: if you do not have enough intelligence to play
> > these game fairly well - don't play; if you do have the intelligence,
> > don't play anyway - use it for something more productive.
> 
> > |  Alexander Chislenko | sasha@cs.umb.edu
> 
> Ah, but this advice applies to almost anything that is not clearly
> "productive"...watching movies, reading fiction, eating at fine
> restaurants, conversing with friends, making love, and even writing
> messages to this list. Imagine this message:
> 
> "My current opinion is: if you do not have enough intelligence to
> write interesting messages to the list - don't write; if you do have
> the intelligence, don't write anyway - use it for something more
> productive."

Yes and no. I actually tend to agree with Sasha on this one. There are
a number of activities I've dropped doing because they are time-sinks
-- watching TV, for example. I haven't watched any TV or even owned a
set in about four years. I'm a lot happier for it, by the way. (My one
regret is ironically not being up on the current advertising, which is
a big indicator of current cultural forces. I'm honestly thinking of
buying videocassettes of current ads from the Ad Bank once in a while
and watching them periodically just so I know whats in vogue.)

I've found that there are certain activities that are simply more
valuable than others. For example, talking to friends is a valuable
activity. On the other hand, when I play games with friends, both
sides tend to spend all their spare brain cycles thinking about the
game and very few communicating with the other person, which is a lot
more important to me. As a result, I've largely stopped playing games
because I enjoy talking more.

This is true of a lot of things in one's life. There are limited
numbers of hours in the day and way too many things to pack in them.
As a result, I've intentionally eliminated certain low-value
activities from my life to replace with higher value ones. I don't go
to movies I know are mediocre any more (I stick to the ones I suspect
to be good and I'm usually right). I still read a lot, but I got rid
of the TV. I write far fewer messages and participate in far fewer
mailing lists than I used to (I no longer read libernet-d, for
instance.) Overall, I've made conscious decisions to free up my day
for the things I want to do, and I feel better for it.

Now, Tim is semi-retired, so he has a lot more hours in the day
available for discretionary activity. I can see why he might like
participating in many more activities than I can afford to. Perhaps
after I've made my fortune I'll have that luxury. For the moment, I
find that a key time management skill is picking and choosing my
activities.

Perry


