Date: Wed, 12 May 93 11:39:01 EDT
From: pmetzger@lehman.com (Perry E. Metzger)
Subject: First CDs, then...
X-Reposting-Policy: redistribute only with permission

As many of you know, the CD market is about to be revolutionized -- a
joint venture of IBM and Blockbuster plans to start building kiosks
that can make CDs on the fly, print the little booklet for the inside
of the jewelbox, shrink wrap it and spit it out at you. This will
mean, of course, that inventory will be a thing of the past, even a
tiny CD shop will have a better selection than a current HMV or Tower
store, and that you will never ever see you favorite artist go out of
print.

I've been waiting for some years to see the same thing happen to
books. I know, we are supposed to get rid of paper, but paper has lots
of advantages to it, not the least of which are compactness and
cheapness, and I suspect physical books will be with us for at least
several more decades. I'm often shocked at how little is in print when
I want it (every bit of libertarian SF ever mentioned on this list I
found out of print when I looked for it, including L. Neil Smiths
books). It should be possible, with some small advances in printing
technology, to produce paperbacks on request in small kiosks. Building
this would, of course, mean that no books would ever go out of print
again or be difficult to find again. (This would be a good business
venture -- hint hint.)

Of course, in the ultimate system, you will simply download albums and
books at will into your multimedia storage rack and play/read them at
will -- no more stores, the network is the store. Books might be read
on small slates the thickness of a piece of cardboard which weigh next
to nothing and have resolution and texture on their surface displays
good enough that you can't distinguish the surface from good quality
printed paper until it magically changes to reveal the next page. TV
networks are a silly idea -- why package together dozens of shows for
sequential viewing when you can just buy what you want when you want?
Purchase the evening news when you want to see it from any one of
dozens of news providers, download the program possibly into the same
slate you read from, and watch the video when you like.

With ubiquitous worldwide networking including wireless nets you could
access your own library or virtual networked book/audio/video sellers
at will from literally anywhere -- in your home or on the beach in
Tahiti you will have exactly the same access. These are developments
are all, of course, a way off, but imagine what they will do to the
economics of the current information delivery businesses like
newspapers, TV networks, bookstores, CD stores, etc.

Perry

